    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a lineup anchored by three high-efficiency offensive threats. **Yordan Alvarez** (74.97) remains our premier play, benefiting from a +7% platoon edge and a favorable matchup against Avila. Close behind, **Junior Caminero** (74.72) is poised for a massive outing; facing a vulnerable Sam Aldegheri (5.47 xERA) while occupying the #3 hole gives him an elite ceiling. Similarly, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (71.57) warrants a \"Strong\" start designation. Despite facing a decent arm in Roupp, his role at the top of the order combined with a significant +14% platoon adjustment makes him a critical piece for our pursuit of SB efficiency, our primary scarcity target.\n\nStrategic adjustments were required to navigate today\u2019s tougher pitching matchups. **Trea Turner** (50.39) and **Kyle Schwarber** (49.63) see their scores suppressed by the elite 2.79 xERA of Misiorowski, pushing them near the fringe of our starting threshold; however, their leadoff/secondary order roles provide enough volume to keep them ahead of bench alternatives. Our most difficult decision involved **Colt Keith**, who posted a respectable 56.96 but ultimately yielded to the superior platoon and park factors surrounding **Caleb Durbin** (60.06). **Caleb Durbin**\u2019s success against Leiter is bolstered by a +7% park factor and the added value of his high-upside speed profile against a pitcher with a slow delivery, which optimizes our SB category chase.\n\nInvestors should remain wary of the volatility surrounding our \"Lineup Pending\" designations for **Casey Schmitt**, **Yordan Alvarez**, and **Pete Crow-Armstrong**. Should any of these players move down the order or get squeezed from the lineup, their efficiency scores will crater. Furthermore, while **Christian Yelich** (47.61) makes the cut, he faces a negative platoon disadvantage against Banks, making him a candidate for mid-game substitution if our defensive alignment requires a shift. Monitor the weather reports closely for any late-breaking wind shifts, as **Carson Benge** (60.24) is currently being buoyed by favorable wind conditions at his park; any shift toward a dead-in breeze would significantly diminish his projected output.",
